NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000023 "__END__"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000024 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000025 "=head1 NAME"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000026 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000027 "Data::UUID - Globally/Universally Unique Identifiers (GUIDs/UUIDs)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000028 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000029 "=head1 SEE INSTEAD?"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000030 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000031 "The module L<Data::GUID> provides another interface for generating GUIDs."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000032 "Right now, it relies on Data::UUID, but it may not in the future.  Its"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000033 "interface may be just a little more straightforward for the average Perl"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000034 "programmer."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000035 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000036 "=head1 SYNOPSIS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000037 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000038 "  use Data::UUID;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000039 "  "
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000040 "  $ug    = Data::UUID->new;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000041 "  $uuid1 = $ug->create();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000042 "  $uuid2 = $ug->create_from_name(<namespace>, <name>);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000043 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000044 "  $res   = $ug->compare($uuid1, $uuid2);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000045 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000046 "  $str   = $ug->to_string( $uuid );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000047 "  $uuid  = $ug->from_string( $str );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000048 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000049 "=head1 DESCRIPTION"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000050 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000051 "This module provides a framework for generating v3 UUIDs (Universally Unique"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000052 "Identifiers, also known as GUIDs (Globally Unique Identifiers). A UUID is 128"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000053 "bits long, and is guaranteed to be different from all other UUIDs/GUIDs"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000054 "generated until 3400 CE."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000055 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000056 "UUIDs were originally used in the Network Computing System (NCS) and later in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000057 "the Open Software Foundation's (OSF) Distributed Computing Environment."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000058 "Currently many different technologies rely on UUIDs to provide unique identity"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000059 "for various software components. Microsoft COM/DCOM for instance, uses GUIDs"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000060 "very extensively to uniquely identify classes, applications and components"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000061 "across network-connected systems."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000062 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000063 "The algorithm for UUID generation, used by this extension, is described in the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000064 "Internet Draft \"UUIDs and GUIDs\" by Paul J. Leach and Rich Salz.  (See RFC"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000065 "4122.)  It provides reasonably efficient and reliable framework for generating"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000066 "UUIDs and supports fairly high allocation rates -- 10 million per second per"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000067 "machine -- and therefore is suitable for identifying both extremely short-lived"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000068 "and very persistent objects on a given system as well as across the network."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000069 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000070 "This modules provides several methods to create a UUID.  In all methods, C<<"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000071 "<namespace> >> is a UUID and C<< <name> >> is a free form string."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000072 " "
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000073 "   # creates binary (16 byte long binary value) UUID."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000074 "   $ug->create();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000075 "   $ug->create_bin();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000076 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000077 "   # creates binary (16-byte long binary value) UUID based on particular"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000078 "   # namespace and name string."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000079 "   $ug->create_from_name(<namespace>, <name>);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000080 "   $ug->create_from_name_bin(<namespace>, <name>);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000081 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000082 "   # creates UUID string, using conventional UUID string format,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000083 "   # such as: 4162F712-1DD2-11B2-B17E-C09EFE1DC403"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000084 "   # Note that digits A-F are capitalized, which is contrary to rfc4122"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000085 "   $ug->create_str();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000086 "   $ug->create_from_name_str(<namespace>, <name>);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000087 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000088 "   # creates UUID string as a hex string,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000089 "   # such as: 0x4162F7121DD211B2B17EC09EFE1DC403"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000090 "   # Note that digits A-F are capitalized, which is contrary to rfc4122"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000091 "   $ug->create_hex();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000092 "   $ug->create_from_name_hex(<namespace>, <name>);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000093 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000094 "   # creates UUID string as a Base64-encoded string"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000095 "   $ug->create_b64();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000096 "   $ug->create_from_name_b64(<namespace>, <name>);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000097 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000098 "   Binary UUIDs can be converted to printable strings using following methods:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000099 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000100 "   # convert to conventional string representation"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000101 "   $ug->to_string(<uuid>);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000102 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000103 "   # convert to hex string (using upper, rather than lower, case letters)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000104 "   $ug->to_hexstring(<uuid>);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000105 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000106 "   # convert to Base64-encoded string"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000107 "   $ug->to_b64string(<uuid>);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000108 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000109 "   Conversely, string UUIDs can be converted back to binary form:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000110 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000111 "   # recreate binary UUID from string"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000112 "   $ug->from_string(<uuid>);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000113 "   $ug->from_hexstring(<uuid>);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000114 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000115 "   # recreate binary UUID from Base64-encoded string"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000116 "   $ug->from_b64string(<uuid>);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000117 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000118 "   Finally, two binary UUIDs can be compared using the following method:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000119 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000120 "   # returns -1, 0 or 1 depending on whether uuid1 less"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000121 "   # than, equals to, or greater than uuid2"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000122 "   $ug->compare(<uuid1>, <uuid2>);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000123 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000124 "Examples:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000125 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000126 "   use Data::UUID;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000127 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000128 "   # this creates a new UUID in string form, based on the standard namespace"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000129 "   # UUID NameSpace_URL and name \"www.mycompany.com\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000130 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000131 "   $ug = Data::UUID->new;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000132 "   print $ug->create_from_name_str(NameSpace_URL, \"www.mycompany.com\");"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000133 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000134 "=head2 EXPORT"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000135 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000136 "The module allows exporting of several standard namespace UUIDs:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000137 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000138 "=over"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000139 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000140 "=item NameSpace_DNS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000141 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000142 "=item NameSpace_URL"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000143 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000144 "=item NameSpace_OID"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000145 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000146 "=item NameSpace_X500"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000147 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000148 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000149 " "
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000150 "=head1 AUTHOR"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000151 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000152 "Alexander Golomshtok <agolomsh@cpan.org>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000153 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000154 "=head1 SEE ALSO"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000155 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000156 "The Internet Draft \"UUIDs and GUIDs\" by Paul J. Leach and Rich Salz (RFC 4122)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000157 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000158 "=cut"
